# Caterva api-server -- environment configuration.
# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values.
#
# ============================================================================
# THIS FILE IS COMMITTED. THE REAL KEYS GO IN .env, WHICH IS NOT.
# ============================================================================
#
# `.env` is ignored by Science-Agent-Pipeline/.gitignore (lines 64-66: `.env`,
# `.env.*`, `*.env`) -- confirm with
#
#     git check-ignore -v Science-Agent-Pipeline/artifacts/api-server/.env
#
# which reports line 66, `*.env`. The next line of that same .gitignore, 67,
# is `!.env.example`: a deliberate carve-out so a documented-but-empty
# template stays committable. That carve-out is the only reason you are
# reading this file from the repository, and it applies to this filename and
# no other. Rename it and it disappears; paste a key into it and the key ships.
#
# A real key pasted below is a key pushed to the remote and scraped off it.
# GitHub is crawled continuously for exactly this, and a leaked free-tier key
# is usually drained within the hour. So every value here is empty or a
# non-secret default, on purpose. If you are looking at a populated
# .env.example, something has gone wrong -- rotate those keys before doing
# anything else.
#
# ============================================================================
# NOTHING IN THIS PACKAGE READS .env BY ITSELF
# ============================================================================
#
# Checked, not assumed: package.json has no dotenv dependency, and `pnpm
# start` is `node --enable-source-maps ./dist/index.mjs` with no
# `--env-file`. The server reads process.env and nothing else. So copying
# this file to .env accomplishes nothing on its own -- whatever starts the
# server has to load it:
#
#     node --env-file=.env dist/index.mjs     # Node in use here is v24
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a; pnpm start    # any POSIX shell
#     docker run --env-file .env ...          # containers
#
# A variable declared here and left empty is EXPORTED AS AN EMPTY STRING by
# every one of those, which is not the same as unset. That distinction bites
# in exactly one place -- see the key-precedence note in the LLM section.
#
# ============================================================================
# THE ACCOUNT BEHIND EVERY KEY
# ============================================================================
#
# All provider accounts are registered to admin.terrium@gmail.com. That is
# recorded here because a key with no known owner cannot be rotated: when one
# of these leaks or expires, whoever is on call needs to know which inbox the
# reset link goes to without having to guess. An unattributed key in a
# running deployment can only be revoked by deleting the account it belongs
# to, and nobody knows which account that is.
#
# The same accounts, and the literature-source keys the Python engine reads,
# are documented in Science-Agent-Pipeline/.env.example. This file covers the
# api-server's own environment only.


# ============================================================================
# CORE SERVER
# ============================================================================

# Port the API server listens on. REQUIRED, with no default: src/index.ts
# throws "PORT environment variable is required but was not provided." when
# it is missing, and rejects a non-numeric or non-positive value rather than
# quietly listening somewhere else. 3000 is left populated because a port is
# not a credential and caterva-site's .env.example already points at it.
PORT=3000

# development | test | production. Two real effects, both checked:
#   - production drops the pino-pretty transport for JSON lines
#     (src/lib/logger.ts).
#   - test disables the rate limiter (src/lib/rateLimit.ts) so the suite is
#     not throttled by its own volume.
# Anything else behaves as development.
NODE_ENV=development

# pino level: fatal, error, warn, info, debug, trace. Defaults to info when
# unset. Raising it to debug does NOT risk leaking a key: the logger redacts
# req.headers.authorization, req.headers.cookie and res set-cookie, and the
# LLM gateway config sets turn_off_message_logging.
# Affects the api-server only; the Python engine logs on its own terms.
LOG_LEVEL=info


# ============================================================================
# PYTHON BRIDGE
# ============================================================================

# Absolute path to the interpreter for every Python bridge call. OPTIONAL and
# left EMPTY on purpose.
#
# src/lib/python.ts builds ONE candidate list, and these two variables each
# REPLACE it rather than being tried before it:
#
#   CATERVA_PYTHON set  -> that path, and nothing else.
#   else VIRTUAL_ENV set -> $VIRTUAL_ENV/bin/python, and nothing else.
#   else                 -> the repo's .venv/bin/python, then python3.13,
#                           3.12, 3.11, 3.10, then bare python3 last.
#
# Every candidate is ACCEPTED only after python.ts runs it and checks
# sys.version_info plus each required import, so the filename was never what
# made a candidate valid -- which is why bare python3 is allowed at all.
#
# Because setting this replaces the list instead of extending it, a stale
# value is a hard failure and not a slower search. The old value in this file
# was /usr/bin/python3.12, a path that exists on very few machines, so anyone
# copying the template got a broken bridge where auto-detection would have
# worked. Supported range is Python 3.10-3.13 -- ADR 0014.
CATERVA_PYTHON=

# Read only when CATERVA_PYTHON is unset, and then as $VIRTUAL_ENV/bin/python
# as the only candidate -- see above. Normally you do not write this here at
# all: activating a virtualenv exports it for you, which is the intended way
# in, and a value written here overrides the venv the operator activated.
# It is documented because python.ts reads it and anyone debugging
# interpreter selection needs to know that it does.
VIRTUAL_ENV=


# ============================================================================
# LLM PROVIDERS -- FOR LANGUAGE TASKS ONLY
# ============================================================================
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A PROVIDER OR A KEY.
#
# These keys drive ENTITY EXTRACTION and DOMAIN CLASSIFICATION in
# src/lib/llmResolver.ts: turning a typed sentence into an enzyme name, a
# substrate, an organism, and a domain label, which then feed BRENDA/KEGG/
# UniProt lookups. Those are language tasks and a language model is the right
# tool for them.
#
# An LLM is NEVER a source for a parameter VALUE. ADR 0011 -- "An LLM-supplied
# parameter is its own origin, not a `default`", amended 2026-08-06 to
# "resolver now hard-blocks `llm`" -- is the rule, and it is the reason this
# project is worth anything to a lab. A Km that came out of a language model
# is not a measurement. The code used to label such values `origin: "default"`,
# which is a false statement at the API surface; the amendment goes further
# than renaming it, and `unverifiedOriginKeys()` now makes an LLM-supplied
# parameter throw RequiredParametersMissingError before it can reach the
# engine, naming the key for the user to supply instead.
#
# Adding capacity below does not widen what an LLM is allowed to decide. No
# number of keys in this file makes an invented number acceptable.
#
# With no key set at all, the pipeline falls back to the deterministic
# keyword/regex resolver. That is a supported configuration, not a degraded
# one: requestJsonCompletion() returns null rather than throwing, and every
# parameter still has to come from literature or from the user.

# -- Option 1: one named provider -------------------------------------------
#
# Selects a row from LLM_PROVIDERS in src/lib/llmResolver.ts -- one of:
# openai, groq, openrouter, mistral, siliconflow, tokenrouter. That row
# supplies the endpoint URL, the default model, and which env var below is
# read for the bearer token.
#
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY WHEN USING THE LITELLM WATERFALL BELOW. Key precedence in
# getApiKey() is: the named provider's key FIRST, then LLM_API_KEY, then
# OPENAI_API_KEY. A named provider whose key is declared-and-empty therefore
# shadows LLM_API_KEY with an empty string, and an empty key switches the LLM
# path off entirely -- it does not fall through.
LLM_PROVIDER=

# groq -- https://console.groq.com/keys
# Account: admin.terrium@gmail.com
# Free tier. Reports its own remaining quota on every call
# (x-ratelimit-remaining-requests / -tokens, plus reset timers), documented
# at https://console.groq.com/docs/rate-limits -- which is why the waterfall
# can know Groq's headroom before a call fails rather than after.
GROQ_API_KEY=

# openrouter -- https://openrouter.ai/keys
# Account: admin.terrium@gmail.com
# Free models carry a `:free` suffix. Documented limits
# (https://openrouter.ai/docs/api-reference/limits): 20 requests/minute and
# 50/day with no credit purchased, 1000/day once $10 has been bought
# lifetime. Rate-limit headers appear on ERROR responses rather than on every
# call, so its headroom is unknown until something 429s.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# mistral -- https://console.mistral.ai/api-keys
# Account: admin.terrium@gmail.com
# Free tier available. Its rate-limit headers were not read when the gateway
# was written, so artifacts/llm-gateway/quota.py holds no header names for it
# and reports its headroom as UNKNOWN rather than assuming it copies Groq's
# scheme. UNKNOWN is not AVAILABLE, and a guessed header name would produce a
# confident misreading.
MISTRAL_API_KEY=

# siliconflow -- https://cloud.siliconflow.com/account/ak
# Account: admin.terrium@gmail.com
# NOTE: llmResolver.ts records supportsJsonMode: false for this provider, so
# response_format:json_object is not sent to it. Forcing it with
# LLM_FORCE_JSON=true below will make it fail rather than comply.
SILICONFLOW_API_KEY=

# tokenrouter -- https://tokenrouter.io
# Account: admin.terrium@gmail.com
# Itself a router over other providers, which makes it the sensible LAST link
# in the waterfall: when TokenRouter fails, everything behind it has failed.
TOKENROUTER_API_KEY=

# -- Option 2: any OpenAI-compatible endpoint -------------------------------
#
# Works regardless of LLM_PROVIDER. Use it for a local llama.cpp or Ollama,
# or -- the intended use -- for the LiteLLM waterfall below.
LLM_API_KEY=
LLM_API_URL=
LLM_MODEL=

# -- Option 3: the OpenAI fallback ------------------------------------------
#
# OPENAI_API_KEY is the last key getApiKey() looks at, and OpenAI's endpoint
# and gpt-4o-mini are the final defaults when nothing else is set.
# Account: admin.terrium@gmail.com
# Paid. Left empty by default: an unset key makes the LLM path report
# unavailable, which is the honest state, whereas a populated one starts
# spending money on a path that is optional.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# CAREFUL. These two OUTRANK LLM_API_URL and LLM_MODEL -- getApiUrl() and
# getModel() check OPENAI_* first. A leftover OPENAI_API_URL is the quiet way
# to point the server at OpenAI while the .env looks like it is wired to the
# local gateway. Both ship empty for that reason; prefer LLM_API_URL and
# LLM_MODEL, which the waterfall section below uses.
OPENAI_API_URL=
OPENAI_MODEL=

# Force (true) or disable (false) response_format:json_object, as the exact
# string "true" or "false". An explicit value here beats every provider's
# checked default, so LLM_FORCE_JSON=false turns JSON mode OFF even for
# providers that support it -- which is why it is not shipped as "false"
# despite reading like a harmless no-op. Leave EMPTY to use each provider's
# checked default (supportsJsonMode in LLM_PROVIDERS); with no provider
# selected, the fallback is whether the URL contains api.openai.com.
LLM_FORCE_JSON=


# ============================================================================
# THE LITELLM WATERFALL (optional, and the reason the keys above are plural)
# ============================================================================
#
# ../llm-gateway/ runs a LiteLLM proxy in front of all five free-tier
# providers, so one key running out moves to the next instead of failing.
# ../llm-gateway/quota.py decides the order from what the providers report
# about their own remaining quota -- no rate limit is hardcoded anywhere in
# that directory, and a provider that reports nothing is recorded UNKNOWN
# rather than assumed fine.
#
# Set it up:
#
#     pip install 'litellm[proxy]'          # not in requirements.txt
#     export LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#     litellm --config ../llm-gateway/config.yaml
#
# Then wire this server to it with Option 2 above -- no application code
# changes, because the generic OpenAI-compatible path already exists:
#
#     LLM_PROVIDER=                # empty, or its key shadows LLM_API_KEY
#     LLM_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1/chat/completions
#     LLM_API_KEY=<the LITELLM_MASTER_KEY value>
#     LLM_MODEL=caterva-extract
#     OPENAI_API_URL=              # empty, or it outranks LLM_API_URL
#
# 127.0.0.1 and not 0.0.0.0: the proxy holds five upstream keys and has no
# business being reachable off the host. `caterva-extract` is the single
# model_name every deployment in config.yaml answers to, which is what makes
# litellm treat them as one logical model with five backends.
#
# litellm is NOT in requirements.txt: nothing else in this repository needs
# it, and the gateway is an optional deployment rather than a dependency of
# the engine. Without it, leave this whole section empty and the server uses
# whichever single provider is configured above.

# The proxy's own key (config.yaml reads os.environ/LITELLM_MASTER_KEY).
# REQUIRED when the gateway runs, with no default on purpose -- a master key
# with a default value is a master key everybody has, and an open port on a
# laptop would otherwise be an open relay to five upstream accounts.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# The api-server does not read this variable; it reads LLM_API_KEY. It is
# documented here because both processes are usually started from the same
# environment, and because the two values have to match.
LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=

# The gateway's own quota-ledger path (CATERVA_QUOTA_LEDGER) is read by
# quota.py, not by this server, and is documented in
# Science-Agent-Pipeline/.env.example. It is not duplicated here so the two
# files cannot drift.


# ============================================================================
# CACHING
# ============================================================================

# Where the job result cache is written. Optional. Default, from
# src/lib/cache.ts, is `../data/cache.json` relative to the compiled bundle
# -- which is artifacts/api-server/data/cache.json for the normal `pnpm dev`
# path, NOT src/data/. Both locations are gitignored
# (Science-Agent-Pipeline/.gitignore lines 82-83) because a real cache.json
# holds BRENDA/KEGG-resolved simulation results and is runtime data, not
# source.
#
# The cache is capped at 250 entries and scanned newest-first. Note ADR 0016:
# a cached result serves no per-parameter provenance, so a cache hit is not a
# substitute for a fresh resolve when provenance is what you need.
CACHE_FILE=

# ============================================================================
# DATA PERSISTENCE
# ============================================================================

# Where waitlist signups are written (src/routes/waitlist.ts). Optional;
# defaults to the same data/ directory as the cache. This file contains real
# people's email addresses -- it is gitignored by the same two lines, and
# docs/PRIVACY.md is the statement of what happens to them and how someone
# gets theirs removed. Point this somewhere backed up and not world-readable.
WAITLIST_FILE=

# ============================================================================
# SECURITY
# ============================================================================

# Bearer token for POST /api/metrics/reset (src/routes/metrics.ts).
#   unset  -> the endpoint returns 501 and resets nothing.
#   set    -> Authorization: Bearer <METRICS_ADMIN_TOKEN>, compared for exact
#             equality; anything else returns 403.
#
# Ships EMPTY, which is the safe state: 501 cannot be abused. The old
# placeholder here read like a value someone might leave in place, and a
# guessable admin token is worse than a disabled endpoint. When you do set
# it, make it high-entropy -- e.g. openssl rand -base64 32 -- because the
# only thing standing between a stranger and your metrics history is this
# string.
METRICS_ADMIN_TOKEN=


# ============================================================================
# NOTES
# ============================================================================
#
# - PORT is required; the server throws without it.
# - For the LLM, set exactly one of: LLM_PROVIDER (+ its key), or
#   LLM_API_KEY + LLM_API_URL, or OPENAI_API_KEY. Setting several is not an
#   error -- precedence silently picks one, and the one it picks is the
#   narrowest match, not the one you edited most recently.
# - With no LLM configured the pipeline uses keyword-based domain detection.
#   Parameter values are unaffected either way: they come from literature or
#   from the user, never from a model. ADR 0011.
# - Every variable above except PORT may be left empty. A variable dropped
#   from this file is a server nobody can configure, so the list is checked
#   by a test: artifacts/llm-gateway/test_env_example.py.
